{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TmV1KHrT2489k5D96pDnHd","type":"tool_result","content":"[opus 2026-10-23] Starting: brute-force enumeration of all stable sets for small K (script /work/enum.py), then theory.\n2026-10-23T04:00:22Z [sol] Starting independent structural analysis and small-K enumeration; will coordinate classification here. Please reserve final submission until proof checked.\n2026-10-23T04:00:40Z [sol] Key structure: any internal pair shares a coordinate, since otherwise one dominates other. Internally stable sets either lie in a coordinate slice, or are exactly 3-point noncollinear triangles (need prove no fourth). An entire slice x_i=a necessary for slice-type stable set. Deriving external conditions.\n2026-10-23T04:01:15Z [sol] Classification appears ONLY even K, unique symmetric triangle {(h,h,0),(h,0,h),(0,h,h)}, K=2h. Slice impossible: forced full slice x_i=a, but adjacent allocation y_i=a+1 cannot be dominated (strict gains in other two need total >=2 while slice has just 1 extra). Up triangle vertices m+d e_i: all lattice points >=m undominated -> d=1; then any m_i>0 gives opposite witness, so K=1 only. Down triangle vertices m+h(1-e_i): each cap y_j>=m_j+h,y_k>=m_k+h is undominated; if m_i>0 cap has extra point. Thus m=0, K=2h. Working rigorous structural lemma proof now.\n2026-10-23T04:01:33Z [sol] Structural lemma proof: choose distinct x=(a,b,c), y=(a,b+d,c-d), d!=0. A point z off x1=a sharing a coordinate with both must be z+=(a+d,b,c-d) or z-=(a-d,b+d,c). They share no coordinate, so at most one may occur. Either such z shares a coordinate with a point w on x1=a only when w=x or y. Thus any non-slice internal set is exactly a triangle. Up/down forms follow taking coordinatewise minima. Ready to write full answer unless opus prefers ownership.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1b5f009b-591d-4563-99f7-77b52c9d44a0","uuid":"20017008-4356-4364-8307-554c3a0d8899","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:01:40.433Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"[opus 2026-10-23] Starting: brute-force enumeration of all stable sets for small K (script /work/enum.py), then theory.\n2026-10-23T04:00:22Z [sol] Starting independent structural analysis and small-K enumeration; will coordinate classification here. Please reserve final submission until proof checked.\n2026-10-23T04:00:40Z [sol] Key structure: any internal pair shares a coordinate, since otherwise one dominates other. Internally stable sets either lie in a coordinate slice, or are exactly 3-point noncollinear triangles (need prove no fourth). An entire slice x_i=a necessary for slice-type stable set. Deriving external conditions.\n2026-10-23T04:01:15Z [sol] Classification appears ONLY even K, unique symmetric triangle {(h,h,0),(h,0,h),(0,h,h)}, K=2h. Slice impossible: forced full slice x_i=a, but adjacent allocation y_i=a+1 cannot be dominated (strict gains in other two need total >=2 while slice has just 1 extra). Up triangle vertices m+d e_i: all lattice points >=m undominated -> d=1; then any m_i>0 gives opposite witness, so K=1 only. Down triangle vertices m+h(1-e_i): each cap y_j>=m_j+h,y_k>=m_k+h is undominated; if m_i>0 cap has extra point. Thus m=0, K=2h. Working rigorous structural lemma proof now.\n2026-10-23T04:01:33Z [sol] Structural lemma proof: choose distinct x=(a,b,c), y=(a,b+d,c-d), d!=0. A point z off x1=a sharing a coordinate with both must be z+=(a+d,b,c-d) or z-=(a-d,b+d,c). They share no coordinate, so at most one may occur. Either such z shares a coordinate with a point w on x1=a only when w=x or y. Thus any non-slice internal set is exactly a triangle. Up/down forms follow taking coordinatewise minima. Ready to write full answer unless opus prefers ownership.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TmV1KHrT2489k5D96pDnHd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
