{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJWH5VrhFS8j63yo938L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The problem is solved and submitted. `/work/answer.md` is final, sol and I both marked FINAL on the board, and I wrote `/work/SUBMIT`.\n\n**Answer:**\n- **Q1:** a stable set exists exactly when K is even. For odd K there is none.\n- **Q2:** for K = 2m there is exactly one stable set: {(0,m,m), (m,0,m), (m,m,0)}, the grid version of the symmetric solution.\n\n**Checks:** an exhaustive search over K = 2 to 14 (`/work/enum.py`) found exactly this pattern. `check.py` confirms the set is stable for K = 2, 4, 6, 10 and 20.\n\n**How the proof in `answer.md` works:**\n1. **Key lemma:** two different allocations avoid dominating each other exactly when they agree in some coordinate. Because the shares always add up to K, any pair that differs in all three coordinates has two differences of the same sign, so one dominates the other.\n2. **Shape of a candidate set:** this forces any set with no internal domination into one of two shapes:\n   - all of it lies on one \"line\" where a single player's share is fixed; or\n   - it is exactly three points of the form R + u·e_i, a small triangle.\n3. **Lines never work:** external stability forces the whole line into the set, and then the allocation one unit above the line can't be dominated. On the continuum some of these lines are stable (the discriminatory solutions); on the grid none survive.\n4. **Most triangles fail:** for triangles pointing outward (u > 0), and for inward ones unless every corner of R equals the triangle's size s, I exhibit an allocation that nothing in the set dominates. The one surviving case forces K = 2s, which is the symmetric set.\n5. **Existence for even K:** an allocation outside the symmetric set that nothing in it dominates would need two shares of at least m, and that only happens for points already in the set.\n\nSol reviewed the draft and fixed one real error in the inward-triangle case: I had written the centre as Q = R + s(1,1,1) when it should be Q = R. Sol also dropped a remark that overstated which lines are stable on the continuum. I re-checked both fixes before submitting."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":358,"cache_read_input_tokens":31576,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":358,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":715,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1b5f009b-591d-4563-99f7-77b52c9d44a0","uuid":"efe4c346-6974-45e3-9d7b-caa6af3ca0e1","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:04:03.766Z","request_id":"req_011CgJWH58XhbvZxrgftvmbp"}
